From: David J. Vandenburgh <david@vandenburgh.co>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: qcom: apr: set the GPR callback before probing the driver

apr_device_probe() only stores the driver's gpr_callback in the service
once the driver's probe has returned, and gpr_do_rx_callback() silently
drops a packet for a service whose callback is not set. A response to
anything a driver sends from its own probe can therefore never arrive.

q6apm does exactly that: apm_probe() sends APM_CMD_GET_SPF_STATE and
waits 5 seconds for the response. Every boot of an AudioReach device
logs

  qcom-apm gprsvc:service:2:1: CMD timeout for [1001021] opcode

5 seconds into that probe, and the sound card is registered 5 seconds
late. On a Retroid Pocket 6 the ADSP is up at 3.3 s and the card
appeared at 10 s. Asking again every 250 ms for those 5 seconds never
got an answer either, while the same query from q6prm's probe, once
q6apm's had returned, was answered at once.

Set the callback before calling probe, and clear it again if probe
fails.

Signed-off-by: David J. Vandenburgh <david@vandenburgh.co>
---
diff --git a/drivers/soc/qcom/apr.c b/drivers/soc/qcom/apr.c
index ea7f839..f0cb1ed 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/qcom/apr.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/qcom/apr.c
@@ -367,9 +367,16 @@ static int apr_device_probe(struct device *dev)
 	struct apr_driver *adrv = to_apr_driver(dev->driver);
 	int ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * The callback has to be in place before probe runs: a driver that
+	 * sends a command from its probe and waits for the response (q6apm
+	 * asks for the APM state) would otherwise never see it, because
+	 * gpr_do_rx_callback() drops packets for a service with no callback.
+	 */
+	adev->svc.callback = adrv->gpr_callback;
 	ret = adrv->probe(adev);
-	if (!ret)
-		adev->svc.callback = adrv->gpr_callback;
+	if (ret)
+		adev->svc.callback = NULL;
 
 	return ret;
 }
